Telescoping Series
The technique demonstrated in this section is called telescopic cancelling.
Like a telescope, the middle section collapses (by cancelling) leaving just the outer terms to be computed.



                  Related Topics 



The first step in many telescoping problems involves Partial Fractions decomposition. This technique is also essential for Integration by Partial Fractions.



Example 2.1
Find the constants a and b such that \dfrac{1}{k(k+1)}=\dfrac{a}{k} + \dfrac{b}{k+1}, for all k\in \mathbb{N}.
Hence, prove that \dfrac{1}{1\times2}+\dfrac{1}{2\times3}+\dfrac{1}{3\times 4}+\dots +\dfrac{1}{n(n+1)}=\dfrac{n}{n+1}



                  Solution 







Example 2.2
Prove that \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1}+\sqrt{2}}+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}+\sqrt{3}}+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}+\sqrt{4}}+\dots+\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{15}+\sqrt{16}}=3



